And what I'd love to
hear from you, Matt,

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:08.920
in your research, what are you
seeing happen in the industry

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:11.860
and what do you think is
driving this adoption?

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:13.720
MATT ASLETT: Yeah,
it's definitely real

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:16.240
and definitely happening.

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:18.280
What's interesting, as
you said, what's driving

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:20.930
the adoption initially--

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:23.840
so we see-- so amongst
surveying our user base,

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:26.300
it's something like
60% of cloud adopters

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:28.910
are using multiple clouds.

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:32.000
A lot of them have ended
up using multiple clouds

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.775
completely by accident
because they've

00:11:33.775 --> 00:11:35.650
got different groups in
the organization that

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:37.691
have used different clouds
of different purposes,

00:11:37.691 --> 00:11:39.710
Shadow IT have just
spun up things.

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:41.750
And they've sort of
stumbled into this world

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:45.500
of having multiple clouds.

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:50.170
And that's not something
that is necessarily

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:51.680
viable in the long term.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:54.229
So what we see increasingly
is organizations doing is--

00:11:54.229 --> 00:11:55.770
I mean, hybrid cloud
is a term that's

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:57.728
used by different people
for different reasons.

00:11:57.728 --> 00:11:59.350
We use that to talk
about companies

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:01.870
actually strategically,
deliberately

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:04.090
using multiple
clouds, specifically

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:07.510
to support a single
application or workload.

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:09.760
And so what we see is
increasingly the organizations

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:10.960
are deliberately doing that.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:15.010
Actually, of that 60% who
are using multiple clouds,

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:18.640
about 70% are either already
using multiple clouds

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.400
to deliver a single
application or are considering

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.620
or are working towards that.

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:26.467
And so increasingly,
as I say, that's

00:12:26.467 --> 00:12:27.550
just going to be the norm.

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:31.450
And it's then about not
comparing different cloud

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:33.940
providers at a very high
level, very strategic,

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:36.790
looking at different
technologies individually

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:39.840
and how you can
combine them to deliver

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.670
a better complete whole.

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:45.355
I mean, a great example of that
would be existing applications,

00:12:45.355 --> 00:12:46.480
they're already running up.

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:49.210
They're in one
cloud, but you want

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:53.500
to take advantage of some
vision analysis machine learning

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:55.420
or speech analysis
machine learning.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:57.330
And it could be a
service on another cloud

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:59.770
is the best choice
for that, so call out

00:12:59.770 --> 00:13:01.827
to that for that
very specific purpose

00:13:01.827 --> 00:13:03.160
to deliver that combined entity.

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:07.750
So we see many, many
organizations are increasingly,

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:09.302
strategically,
deliberately looking

00:13:09.302 --> 00:13:11.260
to do that as opposed to
just sort of stumbling

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:13.612
into the case of
having multiple clouds.

00:13:13.612 --> 00:13:15.070
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY:
Yeah, good point.

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:16.450
Anything you want
to add, Sudhir?

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:19.360
SUDHIR HASBE: Yeah, so the other
thing is multi-cloud is one,

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:23.500
and we fundamentally believe
most of the investments today

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:25.840
for customers are on premises.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:28.780
And so having this
hybrid scenario, where

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:32.770
most customers will be in a
multi-cloud or on-premises

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:34.580
and cloud pattern.

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:38.680
So this is why if you looked
at the keynotes the first two

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:41.950
days, the focus on being an
open cloud and ability for you

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:44.470
to run your workloads
in multiple clouds,

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:47.800
enabling that kind of capability
is super important for us,

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:50.077
ability to run your
Spark workloads,

00:13:50.077 --> 00:13:51.910
whether you're running
on premise, in cloud.

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:54.550
With Beam, what we are
doing, having the same code

00:13:54.550 --> 00:14:00.580
that can run with DataFlow but
also run with Spark or Flink,

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:01.600
super important for us.

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:04.660
So we're focusing a lot
on a standardization.

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:08.140
Supporting standard SQL is
part of that whole initiative

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:09.064
for us--

00:14:09.064 --> 00:14:09.730
super important.

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:11.800
And also Composer,
which is Airflow,

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:13.540
we're taking open
source technology,

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:15.850
making it available
for customers to use.

00:14:15.850 --> 00:14:18.430
And you can use
Airflow or Composer now

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:20.470
to go ahead and
have orchestration

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:23.817
that can span across clouds
on premises, all of that.

00:14:23.817 --> 00:14:25.150
And you can centrally manage it.

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:27.820
So, yes, the open
cloud initiative

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:30.670
is key driver because
of the requirement

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:33.440
that customers have that
spans across multiple clouds

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:33.960
and that.

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:35.110
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:36.940
And then from a
workload perspective,

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:38.830
what are you seeing
customers keep

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:40.630
on prem versus on the cloud?

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:43.050
Is there a difference?

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:44.800
SUDHIR HASBE: Every
customer is different.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:48.070
People are going to choose
what best technologies

00:14:48.070 --> 00:14:50.500
that they need for
their scenarios.

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:55.390
What I am noticing is, from
a Google Cloud perspective,

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:58.180
data analytics, machine learning
is our key differentiator.

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:01.660
And I will see a lot of
times customer first try out

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:05.980
those services on Google and
then they go adopt other areas

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:07.360
and stuff like that.

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:10.990
So I do see a lot of customers
starting with data analytics

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:11.950
on one cloud.

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:14.726
They do have different workloads
that run in different places.

00:15:14.726 --> 00:15:16.600
A lot of people run
their operational systems

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:18.520
on premises, maybe
in other clouds.

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:20.740
But for analytics,
machine learning,

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:22.790
I think we have highly
differentiated services.

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:23.290
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY:
I think so too.

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:24.040
I think there's a
lot of announcements.

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:25.780
SUDHIR HASBE: And that enables
them to leverage these things.

00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:26.365
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:26.365 --> 00:15:27.948
MATT ASLETT: Could
I just add to that?

00:15:27.948 --> 00:15:29.097
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY: Yeah.

00:15:29.097 --> 00:15:31.180
MATT ASLETT: Yeah, I think
one of the things we're

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:32.830
increasingly seeing,
obviously, as you said,

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:34.510
the vast majority
of workloads still

00:15:34.510 --> 00:15:37.240
run on premises, particular
data and analytics workloads.

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:40.720
But what we've seen in
the surveys that we do

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:43.870
is we look at which are the
most popular applications

00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:45.220
or workloads in the cloud.

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:48.159
Obviously database and
analytics are widely popular

00:15:48.159 --> 00:15:48.700
applications.

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:50.408
So they were always
there or thereabouts.

00:15:50.408 --> 00:15:52.892
But they were always somewhat
further down the list.

00:15:52.892 --> 00:15:55.100
In the last couple of years,
they've been increasing.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:57.740
And now I think the last one we
did at the start of this year,

00:15:57.740 --> 00:15:59.410
databases was number
one, analytics

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:01.720
was number two, as
you would expect,

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:05.172
given the wide adoption
of those workloads.

00:16:05.172 --> 00:16:07.005
But it does speak to,
I think, the maturity,

00:16:07.005 --> 00:16:10.360
the increasing maturity
of adoption in relation

00:16:10.360 --> 00:16:11.660
to the cloud.

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:12.540
But you're right.

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:16.690
A key point is there
are a huge amount

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:23.950
of workloads that will either
remain on premises or will be--

00:16:23.950 --> 00:16:26.480
we tend to think about
lifting and shifting workloads

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:27.050
to the cloud.

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:29.175
And actually, most of the
time that doesn't happen.

00:16:29.175 --> 00:16:31.180
It's about refactoring
or redesigning

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:34.600
the applications for the
new version of that running

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:36.550
in the cloud in a
modern architecture,

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:38.420
taking advantage of
newer capabilities.

00:16:38.420 --> 00:16:40.330
So we see a lot more
of that going on.

00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:43.810
And so, in some ways,
it will take a long time

00:16:43.810 --> 00:16:46.224
for some of the changes
to become obvious,

00:16:46.224 --> 00:16:48.640
because a lot of those workloads
will continue on premises

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:50.690
and slowly be retired.

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:52.750
And so, yeah, the shift is--

00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:54.880
it's a fundamental
shift, but it's actually

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:57.370
happening, in some
cases, relatively slowly.

00:16:57.370 --> 00:16:58.120
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY:
Yeah, and I think--

00:16:58.120 --> 00:16:59.330
JORDAN TIGANI: Can I
add one thing to that?

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:00.350
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY:
Yeah, of course.

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:01.059
Go ahead, Jordan.

00:17:01.059 --> 00:17:02.433
JORDAN TIGANI:
Just, in terms of,

00:17:02.433 --> 00:17:04.229
you mentioned that
lift and shift is not

00:17:04.229 --> 00:17:06.020
necessarily something
that people are doing

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:07.270
and they're not starting with.

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:09.700
I think what we often see
is people have a problem,

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:13.150
and that problem may not be
their central data warehousing

00:17:13.150 --> 00:17:14.170
or database team.

00:17:14.170 --> 00:17:16.869
But it may just be a team that
is not getting the numbers they

00:17:16.869 --> 00:17:18.200
want fast enough.

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:20.200
And so they pull
that into the cloud.

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:21.880
And then people start
seeing, hey, wow,

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:24.160
we get these amazing
numbers super quickly.

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:25.420
And they start to kind of--

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:28.480
like it's like a thread in a
sweater that just sort of pulls

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:30.310
more and more, more
and more into it.

00:17:30.310 --> 00:17:32.260
And then eventually the
data warehousing team

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:35.050
is like, hey, all our
data is there already,

00:17:35.050 --> 00:17:38.312
why don't we just move
the rest of our workloads?

00:17:38.312 --> 00:17:39.770
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:42.610
And I think one of the things
you gentlemen have mentioned

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:45.460
earlier was just this idea
of just the massive amounts

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:45.960
of data.

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:49.030
And it starts to make sense
that, for organizations, it's

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:50.800
easier to have all
your data in one place

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:52.690
and then to start
to do analytics.

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:56.375
And so that's
definitely another push.

00:17:56.375 --> 00:17:58.000
SUDHIR HASBE: Just
one more thing, just

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:01.390
in that same realm-- so this
is exactly why, from a product

00:18:01.390 --> 00:18:05.230
perspective, we have invested
in data transfer service.

00:18:05.230 --> 00:18:07.510
So if you think about
it, a lot of customers

00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:09.730
spend a lot of money with
Google on advertising

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:10.900
and different things.

00:18:10.900 --> 00:18:13.400
And our thing was, this is a
completely different audience.

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:14.941
It's not a data
warehousing audience,

00:18:14.941 --> 00:18:16.630
but it is the
marketing analysts that

00:18:16.630 --> 00:18:20.020
need to go in and analyze their
spend, optimize their spend.

00:18:20.020 --> 00:18:23.120
How do you do better
for marketing analytics?

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:27.190
And so we basically launched
data transfer service was like,

00:18:27.190 --> 00:18:29.740
hey, with a few clicks, you
can get your AdWords data,

00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:32.620
DoubleClick data, all of
this data into BigQuery

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:33.880
and then you can analyze it.

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:36.640
We have seen amazing amount
of adoption for this.

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:40.270
Customers who are not
traditionally GCP customers,

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:42.760
knowing this, they're like,
OK, let me with a few clicks

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:45.384
pull data in, start
analyzing it, visualizing it.

00:18:45.384 --> 00:18:47.300
They're creating these
reports in Data Studio,

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:49.160
sharing it across
the organization.

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:51.430
And suddenly, as
Jordan was saying,

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:53.400
you get this whole
community started

00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:55.930
forming in organizations
that started using it

00:18:55.930 --> 00:18:58.394
and then it pulls
through the whole thing.

00:18:58.394 --> 00:18:59.560
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY: OK, great.

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:01.690
Again, guys, we have
a couple of mics,

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:04.300
so if you have any questions
or if you have any comments,

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:06.880
just feel free to come
to one of the mics.

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:09.361
OK, so we'll switch gears
again just a little bit.

00:19:09.361 --> 00:19:11.860
I think one of the things that
I've heard in this conference

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:13.750
over and over again
is this idea of just

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:17.560
open source and open
source architecture, right?

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:21.370
And just, Sudhir,
quickly, what are

00:19:21.370 --> 00:19:24.010
you hearing from our customers
about open source technologies

00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:27.320
and how are they appreciating
what Google Cloud is doing?

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:29.860
SUDHIR HASBE: Yeah, I think
open source is super critical.

00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:32.650
As I talk to customers,
this ability-- just because

00:19:32.650 --> 00:19:34.770
of hybrid and
multi-cloud scenarios,

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:38.530
ability for them to run their
workloads across and having

00:19:38.530 --> 00:19:41.590
that option of running
them anywhere else

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:43.084
is super important for them.

00:19:43.084 --> 00:19:45.250
This is why, as I was saying,
our investment in Beam

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:47.350
is so important
for our customers.

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:48.640
They love DataFlow.

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:50.830
Once customers come,
start leveraging DataFlow

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:54.590
for large-scale data processing,
I hope they never go away.

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:56.680
But that option
always exists, as hey,

00:19:56.680 --> 00:19:58.780
with Beam being open
source and runners being

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:03.220
available in different engines,
they can actually do that.

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:04.920
So that's super
important for them.

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:07.300
It resonates really
well with CIOs and all.

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:10.150
Now similarly, as I was
saying, a lot of customers

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:14.620
already have huge deployments
of Hadoop Spark on premises.

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:17.580
Airflow-- Airflow is an amazing.

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:19.810
Composer, its very
fascinating for me.

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:23.572
Composer, in its private beta
or private alpha we had--

00:20:23.572 --> 00:20:24.905
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY: Private alpha.

00:20:24.905 --> 00:20:26.113
SUDHIR HASBE: Private alpha--

00:20:26.113 --> 00:20:27.670
it was like 1,000 customers.

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:30.670
I have no idea how products
get to 1,000 customers

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:32.540
with being private.

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:34.780
But it was because
Airflow, which

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:38.870
is like the orchestration
tool, Apache Project,

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:40.000
everybody loved it already.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:42.460
So we just enabled
that on the platform,

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:43.700
and that option was crazy.

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:47.350
So there's just supporting open
source, being an open cloud,

00:20:47.350 --> 00:20:49.626
enabling experiences
that can come

00:20:49.626 --> 00:20:51.625
from those is super
important for organizations.

00:20:51.625 --> 00:20:53.620
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY: Yeah, and
Composer GA'd, is that correct?

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:55.990
SUDHIR HASBE: Yeah, we
launched Composer to GA now.

00:20:55.990 --> 00:20:57.365
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY:
Yeah, we'd love

00:20:57.365 --> 00:20:59.470
to hear your thoughts,
Matt, on just open source

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:00.370
and open source technology.

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:01.620
MATT ASLETT: Yeah, absolutely.

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:05.080
I actually-- so I
joined 451 Research

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:06.250
about 10 and bit years ago.

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:07.600
If I can remember that far.

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:10.016
And actually, at that time,
we had an open source practice

00:21:10.016 --> 00:21:12.345
because open source was
relatively new and different.

00:21:12.345 --> 00:21:13.720
And a lot of
companies were still

00:21:13.720 --> 00:21:15.178
trying to figure
out what it meant.

00:21:15.178 --> 00:21:17.620
And so we would work with
clients, both on the enterprise

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:20.120
side and the vendor side, just
to help them figure that out.

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:22.630
Obviously, over time, it
became more normalized,

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:24.590
and it's just part
of everything we do.

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:27.400
But what's been fascinating
is, over that time,

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:31.100
to see the approach
to open source change.

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:32.725
I mean, people used
to say you wouldn't

00:21:32.725 --> 00:21:34.250
get fired for buying IBM.

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:36.250
Now I definitely think
now it's you wouldn't get

00:21:36.250 --> 00:21:37.920
fired for buying open source.

00:21:37.920 --> 00:21:41.290
It's that insurance policy
that organizations have.

00:21:41.290 --> 00:21:44.655
And then most of the engagement
we do with the clients,

00:21:44.655 --> 00:21:48.580
when they're looking at
different products and vendors,

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:51.710
it's not always a deal breaker,
but it's always up there

00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:53.150
in the mix, like
where they would

00:21:53.150 --> 00:21:55.994
default to open if they can.

00:21:55.994 --> 00:21:57.410
And actually, I
had a conversation

00:21:57.410 --> 00:22:00.680
with a Google Cloud
customer the other day

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:03.069
here, which proved
the value of that.

00:22:03.069 --> 00:22:05.360
I can't say who it was, but
they're a very happy Google

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:07.380
Cloud customer.

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:11.790
But for one reason or another,
their Stackdriver costs

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:15.230
suddenly went absolutely
through the roof.

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:17.900
And so what they decided to do
was, just for a brief period,

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:19.670
while they figure
out what's going on,

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:21.650
stop that, switch
to open source.

00:22:21.650 --> 00:22:23.860
Obviously, they had the
skills to be able to do this.

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:25.160
But they could do that.

00:22:25.160 --> 00:22:27.493
They could just switch to
open source, do it themselves,

00:22:27.493 --> 00:22:28.780
because it's open source.

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:30.170
It's a one-off thing.

00:22:30.170 --> 00:22:32.390
They'll probably go back to
using the hosted version.

00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:36.190
But it just was that benefit
that gives people the choice

00:22:36.190 --> 00:22:41.169
to change how they deploy and
manage that, depending on what

00:22:41.169 --> 00:22:42.460
whatever happens in the future.

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:44.630
And it's a little bit of an
insurance policy basically.

00:22:44.630 --> 00:22:45.588
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY: Yeah.

00:22:45.588 --> 00:22:48.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

